  "source": "Windows itself: the known folders and their CSIDL and FOLDERID names and environment variables (Microsoft's Known Folders and CSIDL documentation), the programs and libraries Windows installs under its system folders, the registry keys every Windows installation has (the Explorer shell-folder keys, the Run keys, the Winlogon and Services keys), and the element names of the Task Scheduler task definition schema. Nothing here is about one sample, family or run. Written for this project, not copied from a third-party table.",
  "about": "Host values every Windows system holds or every program may use, which a value the analysis states is refused as: an indicator on one matches every host. Compared case-insensitively, a path with '/' read as '\\'. 'known_folders' are folder names (a component of a path) and 'known_folder_prefixes' the prefixes of the CSIDL and FOLDERID names; 'environment_folders' the environment variables that stand for a known folder. A folder or file path whose every component is a known folder, an environment folder, a drive or the profile name after 'Users' is a known folder. 'system_folders' are the folders Windows installs its own programs in; a name of 'system_binaries' is refused bare or under the system folders alone (a drive allowed), and kept anywhere else, where it is a program copied out of place. 'registry_keys' are keys without their hive. 'task_definition_words' are the element and value names of a task definition, which a task name or any other name never is. 'generic_names' are names ordinary software also gives its own tasks, services, files and folders: such a value is exported with a note that it may match ordinary software, and never forms a Sigma selection.",
